version: 3

metadata:
  name: px4_offboard_envelope
  title: PX4 offboard flight envelope on an X500-class quadrotor in roqsim (MuJoCo)
  description: >
    The same physical question as drone_flight_envelope -- does the aircraft hold its commanded
    course as its thrust margin is eaten away? -- flown by a REAL flight stack. A ~2 kg X500-class
    quadrotor is commanded from ROS 2 with px4_msgs offboard setpoints; those reach PX4 SITL over
    uXRCE-DDS, and PX4 flies the airframe in roqsim's MuJoCo physics over the documented Simulator
    MAVLink API on TCP 4560. Nothing that stabilises the aircraft is ours: EKF2, the position and
    attitude controllers, the control allocator and the failsafes are all PX4's, running the code
    an operator flashes onto a vehicle.

    PX4 hovers this airframe at 60% throttle (MPC_THR_HOVER 0.6), so the four rotors together
    deliver 32.7 N against a 19.6 N airframe -- an unloaded thrust-to-weight ratio of 1.67. The
    grid walks payload, air density and wind until that margin is gone.
  keywords: [robotics, aerial, drone, quadrotor, PX4, px4_msgs, offboard, uXRCE-DDS, MAVLink, mujoco, roqsim]

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Factors. A full 3 x 2 x 2 grid = 12 configurations, the same shape as drone_flight_envelope.
#
# Every destination here is a `sim:` path into the roqsim world, resolved against the addresses
# `world/px4_envelope.yaml` publishes:
#   sim.<key>                           -> a pre-compile global (density is one)
#   components.<entity>.<plugin>.<key>  -> a plugin nested under an entity (the drone's payload)
#   components.<plugin>.<key>           -> a top-level plugin (the wind field)
#
# Note what is NOT varied: anything inside PX4. The flight stack is the thing under test, so a
# campaign that retuned MPC_* gains between cells would be measuring our tuning rather than the
# stack's envelope. Every factor below is a property of the aircraft or of the air around it.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
configuration:
- name: envelope
  variations:

  # The headline factor, and the reason this example exists. Total rotor thrust is fixed at 32.7 N
  # by the airframe (files/10020_none_x500: MPC_THR_HOVER 0.6 against a 2.0 kg aircraft, i.e.
  # 19.613 / 0.6), so payload is what moves the margin:
  #
  #   payload   total mass   weight     T/W = 32.7 N / weight
  #   0.0 kg    2.0 kg       19.613 N   1.667
  #   0.6 kg    2.6 kg       25.497 N   1.283
  #   1.2 kg    3.2 kg       31.381 N   1.042
  #
  # The top level sits just above T/W = 1 (the aircraft can barely lift itself; total mass at
  # exactly 1.0 is 3.334 kg) rather than comfortably inside the envelope, so the grid brackets the
  # boundary between two cells instead of straddling it. A factor whose every level passes has not
  # been varied over a range where it does anything.
  #
  # Worth stating plainly because it surprises people: 1.67 is far below the 3+ that a bench figure
  # for X500-class motors would suggest. It is PX4's own hover throttle that sets the scale here --
  # the airframe file says the aircraft hovers at 60% command, so full command is 1/0.6 of a hover,
  # and that is the margin the flight stack believes it has.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      sim: components.drone.payload.mass
      values: [0.0, 0.6, 1.2]

  # Density altitude. 1.225 is sea level, 0.905 is roughly 3000 m. Worth knowing what this factor
  # does and does not do here: MuJoCo's density drives the drag terms, so it scales the force the
  # wind applies and shows up mostly as an *interaction* with the wind factor. It does not change
  # thrust, which a real density altitude would -- the rotors' force limit is fixed -- so payload
  # is the thrust-margin factor and this is not.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      sim: sim.density
      values: [1.225, 0.905]

  # Calm against a 4 m/s cross-wind from the east. Higher than the Crazyflie example's 2.5 m/s and
  # for a reason: a 2 kg airframe with PX4's position controller shrugs that off, so it would be a
  # factor with no effect. Written as a whole vector because a list destination is replaced
  # wholesale -- there is no index addressing in the override path.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      sim: components.wind_field.steady
      values:
      - [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]
      - [4.0, 0.0, 0.0]

execution:
  containers:
    # -- the physics ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # roqsim, from its own image, supplied by the backend. It serves the Simulator MAVLink API on
    # TCP 4560 (world/px4_envelope.yaml, the `px4_sitl` component) and PX4 dials in.
    simulation:
      backend: roqsim
      config: world/px4_envelope.yaml
      # Needs a simulator image carrying `roqsim_aerial` with the `x500` model and the
      # `multirotor_motors`, `px4_sitl` and `gnss` plugins (`payload` is core roqsim). An older
      # image fails at world load with "model 'x500' not found", listing the model providers it
      # searched -- which names the cause precisely enough to act on.
      #
      # No `image:` here on purpose: the backend supplies the family member and the deployment
      # supplies the project (ROBOVAST_PROJECT). A ref in this file would pin a public example to
      # one site's registry.
      #
      # pymavlink is declared HERE rather than carried by the simulator image, for the same reason
      # px4_msgs is not in the framework image: it is needed only by a campaign that attaches a
      # flight stack. roqsim_aerial makes it an optional extra (`roqsim_aerial[px4]`) precisely so a
      # world flying the in-process `quadrotor_controller` -- the majority -- does not pull a
      # MAVLink stack in, and an image installing it unconditionally would undo that one layer up.
      # `px4_sitl` imports it lazily and fails by name, so an image without it says what is missing.
      python_packages: [pymavlink]
      resources: {cpu: 1, cpu_limit: 4, memory: 2048Mi}

    # -- the flight stack ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    # PX4 SITL, in a container of its own. This is the whole point of the example: the stabilising
    # controller is no longer a plugin inside the simulator, it is the autopilot firmware.
    #
    # The image is the PX4 project's own published prebuilt SITL image, which is exactly the right
    # thing to name in a public example -- anyone who clones this can pull it. Pinned to a concrete
    # tag rather than `latest`, because PX4 renames its own ROS 2 topics between releases (see the
    # long note in scenario.osc) and a floating tag would silently change the topic the metrics
    # read. Note honestly that the prebuilt SITL images are pre-release tags only: there is no
    # stable-release prebuilt SITL image at the time of writing.
    px4:
      image: px4io/px4-sitl:v1.18.0-beta2
      provenance:
        source: https://github.com/PX4/PX4-Autopilot
        revision: v1.18.0-beta2
        build_recipe: published by the PX4 project; see https://hub.docker.com/r/px4io/px4-sitl
      # A script, not an argv, and not by taste: a sidecar's command is handed to
      # secondary_entrypoint.sh as one string and word-split unquoted, so no single argument may
      # contain a space. files/px4_start.sh installs the airframe PX4 does not ship (see below),
      # points PX4 at the MAVLink HIL path and execs it.
      command: [/bin/bash, /config/files/px4_start.sh]
      # MEASURED, not precautionary: this image ships `stdbuf` and `tee` but NOT `python3`, and
      # RoboVAST's secondary_entrypoint.sh hard-exits without all three. Declaring it here triggers
      # a derived image build on top of the pinned tag, which is the framework's own mechanism for
      # exactly this -- the alternative would be pinning a different PX4 image for a reason that has
      # nothing to do with PX4.
      system_packages: [python3]
      # PX4 SITL under lockstep is a hard real-time-ish loop and gets a core to itself; it is
      # small in memory. UNVERIFIED as a measurement -- these are estimates, and
      # `get_campaign_summary` reports what the container actually used after the first campaign.
      resources: {cpu: 1, cpu_limit: 2, memory: 512Mi}

    # -- the ROS 2 side ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # The scenario container also runs the uXRCE-DDS agent (started by the scenario with
    # run_process) and carries the px4_msgs interfaces, because that is where the ROS 2 graph is.
    #
    # Both are declared HERE rather than baked into the framework's base image. Neither is
    # installable: checked against ros/rosdistro jazzy/distribution.yaml on 2026-08-31, px4_msgs
    # has a `source:` entry and no `release:` block, and microxrcedds_agent is absent from the
    # distribution entirely -- so `system_packages` (apt) and `python_packages` (pip) cannot
    # express either, and neither is on PyPI. `ros_packages` is the key for exactly that case: it
    # colcon-builds pinned git sources into an overlay in this campaign's derived image. A drone
    # stack in the shared base image would be paid for by every campaign that never flies.
    scenario:
      ros_packages:
      # PX4's ROS 2 message definitions. Pinned to a sha on release/1.18 and deliberately not to
      # `main`: px4_msgs definitions are versioned per PX4 release, and the uXRCE-DDS client
      # derives each topic's `_vN` suffix from the message's own MESSAGE_VERSION -- so interfaces
      # from the wrong branch produce topic names that silently do not match the running
      # autopilot. This tracks the PX4 release the `px4` container above pins. There is no
      # v1.18.0 tag to use instead; PX4 1.18 is still pre-release, which is also why the only
      # prebuilt PX4 SITL images are betas.
      - git: https://github.com/PX4/px4_msgs.git
        ref: 598c7aad7b2386f9406ebd2a2f841619fddc3c78   # release/1.18 HEAD

      # The Micro XRCE-DDS Agent: the bridge PX4's uXRCE-DDS client dials into (UDP 8888 by
      # default), and what puts `/fmu/in/*` and `/fmu/out/*` on the ROS 2 graph.
      #
      # It is NOT a ROS package -- it ships no package.xml -- and it belongs under `ros_packages`
      # anyway: colcon identifies a plain CMake project by its CMakeLists.txt, and this repo
      # carries a `colcon.pkg` giving colcon its name, its `cmake` type, and its cmake arguments.
      # Those arguments are why this is one line rather than a build recipe: they set
      # UAGENT_USE_SYSTEM_FASTDDS / UAGENT_USE_SYSTEM_FASTCDR, so the agent links the Fast DDS the
      # ROS distribution already ships instead of running its superbuild to fetch and compile a
      # second copy of the middleware.
      #
      # The pin is the 2.x line and must stay there until the base ROS distribution moves: agent
      # 3.x requires Fast DDS 3.x, and this distribution ships 2.14, which exports `fastrtps`. A
      # 3.x pin fails at configure with `Could not find a package configuration file provided by
      # "fastdds"` -- loud, but only after someone has spent the build.
      - git: https://github.com/eProsima/Micro-XRCE-DDS-Agent.git
        ref: 73622810d984349b80bbac0ef55fc0b694d62222   # v2.4.3
      resources: {cpu: 1, cpu_limit: 2, memory: 1024Mi}

  # The simulator runs standalone and publishes /clock; PX4 and the scenario are separate
  # containers sharing its network namespace, which is what lets PX4 reach TCP 4560 and the
  # uXRCE-DDS client reach UDP 8888 on plain localhost -- no hostname anywhere in this campaign.
  mode: ros2
  # Three per cell. The turbulence in a run is a function of the run's seed, so repetitions sample
  # the weather rather than repeat it -- which is what makes averaging over them mean anything.
  # EKF2 also converges slightly differently per run, so a repetition now samples the estimator
  # too, which it did not in the Crazyflie example.
  runs: 3
  # The trial schedule is ~95 s of sim time, and PX4 bringup (EKF2 convergence to a GPS fix before
  # it will arm) is another ~30 s that the Crazyflie example simply did not have. 420 is a backstop
  # for a run that wedges, not a bound on the flight: without it a hung run is indistinguishable
  # from a slow one and `get_campaign_status` can return no stall verdict at all.
  timeout: 420
  scenario_file: scenario.osc
  run_files:
  # Per-extension rather than "world/*": a bare glob crosses '/' and would sweep __pycache__ into
  # the config identity hash. These are mounted at /config/<path> in EVERY container, which is how
  # a vanilla PX4 image is handed both its start script and an airframe file it does not ship.
  - "world/*.yaml"
  - "world/*.xml"
  - "files/*.py"
  - "files/*.sh"
  # Named literally: PX4 airframe files carry no extension, so no glob above matches it.
  - "files/10020_none_x500"
  env:
  - PYTHONUNBUFFERED: "1"

results_processing:
  postprocessing:
  # The bag -> CSV step must come first: EnvelopeMetrics reads what it writes. Both position
  # sources, because the estimator error between them is itself a metric.
  - rosbags_to_csv: {topics: [/fmu/out/vehicle_local_position_v1, /drone/odom]}
  - files/metrics.py:EnvelopeMetrics

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visualisation. Carried over from drone_flight_envelope and adapted to the new topics. The roqsim
# `scene3d` panel is added automatically for a roqsim backend and is the centrepiece -- the
# aircraft flying the course, tracked by the world's own camera. Everything below exists so that
# the *numbers driving that picture* are on screen beside it: without them a windy run and a heavy
# one look equally like "the drone wobbled".
#
# One panel is new, and it is the one that only exists because a real flight stack is in the loop:
# the estimator-error trace. In the Crazyflie example there was nothing to compare -- the
# controller read ground truth. Here PX4 flies on what EKF2 believes, and the gap between belief
# and truth is a first-class observable.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
visualization:
  config:
    panels:
    # Before any run: which cell of the grid this is.
    - parameters: {title: Flight envelope, height: "40%"}
    - scene3d: {title: Flight room}

  results:
    run_view:
      panels:
      # The 3D scene is the picture; everything else floats over it. Declared rather than left to
      # the backend's automatic one so it can claim `fill` -- without it the scene takes only what
      # the docks leave.
      - scene3d: {title: Flight room, fill: true}

      # trajectory is written at the simulator's rate, so an undecimated 100 s flight runs to tens
      # of thousands of rows against a 5000-row reply cap that truncates at the HEAD -- every panel
      # would then show the first ten seconds and nothing after. 50 Hz keeps the tilt and speed
      # transients and lands well under the cap.
      #
      # ENU, not NED: this plots the ground truth from /drone/odom, which is the simulator's frame.
      # The NED numbers live in the scenario and in PX4; they are converted once, in metrics.py.
      - scene:
          title: Ground track
          position: {anchor: top-left, width: 360, height: 360}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: trajectory, time_column: t, decimate_hz: 50}
          x: x
          y: y
          trail: true

      # `fields` are objects, not bare column names: the panel reads `column` off each entry, so a
      # plain string renders as a dash -- a row with no name and no value.
      - state:
          title: Flight state
          position: {anchor: top-right, width: 320, height: 220}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: trajectory, time_column: t, decimate_hz: 50}
          fields:
          - {column: z, label: altitude, unit: m}
          - {column: tilt_deg, label: tilt, unit: deg}
          - {column: speed, label: speed, unit: m/s}
          - {column: est_error, label: estimator error, unit: m}
          - {column: x, label: x, unit: m}
          - {column: y, label: y, unit: m}

      - timeseries:
          title: Altitude, tilt and speed
          position: {anchor: bottom-right, width: 460, height: 240}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: trajectory, time_column: t, decimate_hz: 50}
          series:
          - {column: z, label: "altitude [m]"}
          - {column: tilt_deg, label: "tilt [deg]"}
          - {column: speed, label: "speed [m/s]"}

      # New in this example. Ground-truth altitude against what EKF2 believed the altitude was,
      # on one axis. A run where the two diverge is a run whose tracking error is an ESTIMATION
      # problem, not a control or thrust one -- and telling those apart is exactly what a campaign
      # against a real flight stack can do and a campaign against our own PD loop cannot.
      - timeseries:
          title: Truth vs PX4 estimate (altitude)
          position: {anchor: bottom-left, width: 400, height: 240}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: trajectory, time_column: t, decimate_hz: 50}
          series:
          - {column: z, label: "truth [m]"}
          - {column: est_z, label: "EKF2 [m]"}

      # Every container's output merged with /rosout on the playback clock -- and with three
      # containers this is where PX4's own startup and arming refusals appear, which is usually the
      # first place to look when a cell flew nothing.
      - log:
          title: Run log
          position: {anchor: bottom-center, width: "calc(100% - 900px)", height: 200}
          minimizable: true
          minimized: true

      timeline: {table: trajectory, time_column: t}

    data_browser:
      # The only built-in cross-run visual, and the one that shows the envelope as a surface rather
      # than as twelve separate flights. `runs` carries every scenario parameter as `param_*`.
      plots:
      - title: Altitude held vs payload and wind
        query: >
          SELECT r.config_name AS config,
                 m.cruise_hold_fraction AS held,
                 m.mean_altitude_error AS altitude_error,
                 m.max_tilt_deg AS max_tilt,
                 m.mean_estimator_error AS estimator_error,
                 m.outcome AS outcome
          FROM runs r
          JOIN metrics m ON (m.config_name = r.config_name AND m.run_id = r.run_id)
        vega_lite:
          mark: point
          encoding:
            x: {field: altitude_error, type: quantitative, title: "mean altitude error [m]"}
            y: {field: max_tilt, type: quantitative, title: "max tilt [deg]"}
            color: {field: outcome, type: nominal}
            shape: {field: outcome, type: nominal}
      # The estimator, made visible across the grid. If this rises with payload the flight stack is
      # not merely running out of thrust -- its state estimate is degrading with the vibration and
      # attitude excursions that running out of thrust produces.
      - title: Estimator error vs thrust margin
        query: >
          SELECT r.config_name AS config,
                 m.mean_estimator_error AS estimator_error,
                 m.mean_altitude_error AS altitude_error,
                 m.outcome AS outcome
          FROM runs r
          JOIN metrics m ON (m.config_name = r.config_name AND m.run_id = r.run_id)
        vega_lite:
          mark: point
          encoding:
            x: {field: altitude_error, type: quantitative, title: "mean altitude error [m]"}
            y: {field: estimator_error, type: quantitative, title: "mean |truth - EKF2| [m]"}
            color: {field: outcome, type: nominal}
